            <ArticleTitle>The effect of intrathecal gabapentin on neuropathic pain is independent of the integrity of the dorsolateral funiculus in rats.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>837-42</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.lfs.2012.08.032</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0024-3205(12)00482-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study evaluates the contribution of inhibitory pain pathways that descend to the spinal cord through the dorsolateral funiculus (DLF) on the effect of intrathecal gabapentin against spinal nerve ligation (SNL)-induced behavioral hypersensitivity to mechanical stimulation in rats.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Rats were submitted to a sham or complete ligation of the right L5 and L6 spinal nerves and a sham or complete DLF lesion. Next, the changes induced by intrathecal administration of gabapentin on the paw withdrawal threshold of rats to mechanical stimulation were evaluated electronically.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="KEY FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Intrathecal gabapentin (200μg/5μl) that was injected 2 or 7days after surgery fully inhibited the SNL-induced behavioral hypersensitivity to mechanical stimulation in sham DLF-lesioned rats; gabapentin was effective against the SNL-induced behavioral hypersensitivity to mechanical stimulation also in DLF-lesioned rats.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SIGNIFICANCE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The effect of intrathecally administered gabapentin against SNL-induced behavioral hypersensitivity to mechanical stimulation in rats does not depend on the activation of nerve fibers that descend to the spinal cord via the DLF.</AbstractText>
